5	### 4.2 The dynamic choice (§5.2, pp. 11:16-21)
6	
7	- **The contract.** "dynamic overloading resolution then must select an
8	  instance of the most specific method declaration that is applicable to the
9	  dynamic types of the arguments and whose return type is a subtype of the
10	  static type of the expression" (p. 11:15).
11	- **Types at run time.** Types are reified: "all the type parameters are
12	  instantiated with ground types at run time", and a value's run-time type is
13	  its *ilk* (p. 11:17). Ground types include unions and intersections
14	  (Fig. 8, p. 11:17).
15	- **[R-Method]** (p. 11:17). It sorts the visible declarations most-to-least
16	  specific ("This order is statically determinable, so the sorting can be
17	  done at compile time"). It then runs the *dispatch semipredicate* on each
18	  declaration in turn and takes the first that succeeds. The receiver's ilk is
19	  matched together with the arguments' ilks, which is what makes the dispatch
20	  symmetric.
21	- **The semipredicate** ([Dispatch], pp. 11:17-18) has three steps:
22	  1. match the argument ilks against the domain to get lower and upper bounds
23	     for the method type parameters;
24	  2. match the call's static return type against the declared return type to
25	     get bounds "to make sure that the dynamic dispatch preserves type";
26	  3. solve the bounds.
27	
28	  "If any of these steps fails, the semipredicate is false: the declaration
29	  is not applicable" (p. 11:18). Matching a union can yield several candidate
30	  bound lists, combined as a Cartesian product (pp. 11:18-19).
31	- **Solving** (Fig. 10, p. 11:19). The rule works from the rightmost type
32	  parameter to the left and "computes a substitution … by taking the
33	  intersection of the upper bounds", declared and computed. It then
34	  propagates the choice into the declared lower bounds of the parameters to
35	  its left. It "never instantiates method type parameters with Bottom". The
36	  right-to-left pass works because of the binding restrictions (p. 11:11):
37	  - a method type parameter's scope "extends to the right of its
38	    declaration";
39	  - "no reference to a method type parameter appears in any of the upper
40	    bounds";
41	  - the union of the lower bounds must be below the intersection of the upper
42	    bounds.
43	- **The worked example** (§5.2.4, pp. 11:20-21).
44	  - Two covariant `append` declarations, over `SortedList` and over `List`.
45	  - The call has static type `List⟦C3⟧` and argument ilks `SortedCons⟦C1⟧`
46	    and `Cons⟦C2⟧`, with C1, C2 <: C3.
47	  - The `SortedList` declaration fails on the second argument, so the `List`
48	    one runs, instantiated at `[C3/P, C3/Q]`.
49	  - So the instance comes from the static return type's upper bound, not from
50	    the ilks' C1 and C2.
51	- **Why intersection of upper bounds.** The Fortress team "had assumed …
52	  that once the tightest possible upper and lower bounds for a method type
53	  parameter have been found, then the union (or pseudo-union) of the lower
54	  bounds should be chosen". For that and for a hybrid "we were unable to
55	  complete the proofs of correctness", so the algorithm "always chooses the
56	  intersection of the upper bounds" (p. 11:25).
57	- **Union instances.** A type parameter can be instantiated at a union at
58	  run time. In the paper's example `O.m(3, true): (Int ⊔ Boolean)`, "P of m
59	  is inferred as (Int ⊔ Boolean) by the dispatch semipredicate at run time"
60	  (p. 11:21).
